<?xml version="1.0"?>
<section xml:id="algorithm-analysis_big-o-notation">
  <title>Big O Notation</title>
  <p>In the previous section, we compared three algorithms for computing
            the sum of the first <m>n</m> integers. By counting the dominant
            operation&#x2014;the assignment statement&#x2014;instead of measuring
            wall-clock time, we obtained a machine-independent picture of each
            algorithm's efficiency: the iterative version
            (<xref ref="lst-sum1"/>) performed <m>n</m> operations, the
            nested-loop version (<xref ref="lst-sum-nested"/>) performed roughly
            <m>\frac{n^2}{2}</m>, and the closed-form formula
            (<xref ref="lst-sum3"/>) performed just 1. We described these as
            <em>linear</em>, <em>quadratic</em>, and <em>constant</em>
            performance. Now we need a concise, formal notation to express these
            differences&#x2014;one that lets us quickly compare any two algorithms
            without worrying about exact operation counts or machine-specific
            details. That notation is called <term>Big O</term>.</p>
  <p>Before introducing Big O itself, let us first become comfortable
            with <m>T(n)</m>&#x2014;the function that counts the <em>exact</em>
            number of operations an algorithm performs for an input of size
            <m>n</m>. Once you can derive <m>T(n)</m> systematically from code,
            Big O will follow naturally as a simplification.</p>
  <p>Recall the iterative summation function <c>sum_of_n</c>
            (<xref ref="lst-sum1"/>). A good basic unit of computation for this
            algorithm is the assignment statement. The number of assignments is
            1 (<m>the\_sum = 0</m>) plus the value of <em>n</em> (the number of
            times we perform <m>the\_sum = the\_sum + i</m>). We express this
            as a function, call it <m>T</m>, where <m>T(n) = 1 + n</m>. The
            parameter <em>n</em> is the <q>size of the problem,</q> and we read
            <m>T(n)</m> as <q>the number of steps it takes to solve a problem
            of size <m>n</m>.</q></p>
  <p>In the summation functions given above, it makes sense to use the number
            of terms in the summation to denote the size of the problem. We can then
            say that the sum of the first 100,000 integers is a bigger instance of
            the summation problem than the sum of the first 1,000. Because of this,
            it might seem reasonable that the time required to solve the larger case
            would be greater than for the smaller case. Our goal then is to show how
            the algorithm's execution time changes with respect to the size of the
            problem.</p>
  <p>To derive <m>T(n)</m> from any piece of code, follow these four
            rules:</p>
  <p><ol>
    <li><p><em>Simple statements cost 1.</em> Each assignment, comparison,
        arithmetic operation, or return counts as a single step.</p></li>
    <li><p><em>Sequential statements add.</em> If one block takes
        <m>a</m> steps and the next takes <m>b</m> steps, the total is
        <m>a + b</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p><em>A loop multiplies.</em> If a loop runs <m>k</m> times and
        the body costs <m>c</m> steps per iteration, the loop contributes
        <m>k \times c</m> steps.</p></li>
    <li><p><em>Nested loops multiply again.</em> If an outer loop runs
        <m>m</m> times and an inner loop runs <m>k</m> times, the inner
        body executes <m>m \times k</m> times total.</p></li>
  </ol></p>
  <p>Let us apply these rules to a more involved example. Consider the
            fragment of Python code shown in
            <xref ref="algorithm-analysis_lst-dummycode"/>. Although this program
            does not do anything useful, it lets us practice counting operations
            systematically.</p>
  <listing xml:id="algorithm-analysis_lst-dummycode"><program label="algorithm-analysis_lst-dummycode" interactive="activecode" language="python">
    <input>
# Counting assignment operations
# --------------------------------
# This code does not do anything useful, but it lets us practice
# counting the number of assignment operations as a function of n.
# Try changing n and observe how the count changes.

n = 10
count = 0

a = 5;  count += 1
b = 6;  count += 1
c = 10; count += 1
for i in range(n):
    for j in range(n):
        x = i * i; count += 1
        y = j * j; count += 1
        z = i * j; count += 1
for k in range(n):
    w = a * k + 45; count += 1
    v = b * b;      count += 1
d = 33; count += 1

print("n = %d" % n)
print("Total assignments = %d" % count)
print("Formula: 3n^2 + 2n + 4 = %d" % (3*n**2 + 2*n + 4))
</input>
  </program></listing>
  <p>Applying our rules step by step:</p>
  <p><ul>
    <li><p><em>Three assignments before the loops</em> (<c>a = 5</c>,
        <c>b = 6</c>, <c>c = 10</c>): by Rule 1, that is
        <m>3</m> steps.</p></li>
    <li><p><em>The nested loop</em>: the outer loop runs <m>n</m> times,
        the inner loop runs <m>n</m> times, and the body contains 3
        assignments. By Rules 3 and 4:
        <m>n \times n \times 3 = 3n^{2}</m> steps.</p></li>
    <li><p><em>The single loop</em>: runs <m>n</m> times with 2
        assignments in the body. By Rule 3:
        <m>n \times 2 = 2n</m> steps.</p></li>
    <li><p><em>One final assignment</em> (<c>d = 33</c>): by Rule 1,
        <m>1</m> step.</p></li>
  </ul></p>
  <p>By Rule 2, we add everything together:
            <m>T(n) = 3 + 3n^{2} + 2n + 1 = 3n^{2} + 2n + 4</m>. Run the
            code above with different values of <c>n</c> to verify that the
            formula matches the counted total.</p>
  <exercises>
    <title>Self Check</title>
    <exercise label="tn_sc1">
      <statement>
        <p>What is <m>T(n)</m> for the following code?</p>
        <program language="python">
          <input>
total = 0
for i in range(n):
    total = total + i
for j in range(n):
    for k in range(n):
        total = total + 1
          </input>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>T(n) = n + n^{2}</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>You counted the loops correctly, but do not forget the
                        assignment <c>total = 0</c> at the top. Every simple
                        statement counts as 1.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><m>T(n) = n^{2} + n + 1</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct! The initial assignment is 1 step, the first loop
                        contributes <m>n</m>, and the nested loop contributes
                        <m>n \times n = n^{2}</m>. Adding: <m>1 + n + n^{2}</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>T(n) = 2n + 1</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>It looks like you added the two loops as if they were both
                        single loops. The second loop is <em>nested</em>&#x2014;an inner
                        loop of <m>n</m> inside an outer loop of <m>n</m> gives
                        <m>n^{2}</m>, not <m>n</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>T(n) = n^{3}</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Cubic would require three levels of nesting. Here the
                        first loop and the nested loop are <em>sequential</em>
                        (one after the other), so their costs add rather than
                        multiply.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="tn_sc2">
      <statement>
        <p>What is <m>T(n)</m> for the following code?</p>
        <program language="python">
          <input>
x = 0
for i in range(n):
    x = x + 1
y = 0
for j in range(n):
    y = y + 1
          </input>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>T(n) = n^{2}</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Be careful&#x2014;these two loops are <em>sequential</em>,
                        not nested. Sequential loops add; nested loops multiply.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>T(n) = 2n</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>You correctly added the two loops (<m>n + n</m>), but
                        do not forget the two assignment statements
                        <c>x = 0</c> and <c>y = 0</c>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><m>T(n) = 2n + 2</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct! Two initial assignments (2) plus two sequential
                        loops each running <m>n</m> times (<m>n + n = 2n</m>).
                        Total: <m>2n + 2</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>T(n) = n + 2</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>It looks like you only counted one of the two loops.
                        Both loops run <m>n</m> times and are sequential, so
                        their costs add: <m>n + n = 2n</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
  <p>Now that you can systematically derive <m>T(n)</m> from code, the
            next question is: do we really need all those exact terms? Consider
            the expression <m>T(n) = 3n^{2} + 2n + 4</m> from
            <xref ref="algorithm-analysis_lst-dummycode"/>. For small values of
            <m>n</m>, every term matters. But algorithm analysis is concerned
            with how algorithms perform on <em>large</em> data sets, not small
            ones. To see why this matters, consider a simpler question: which
            grows faster, <m>n^{2}</m> or <m>1000n</m>?</p>
  <table xml:id="tbl-n2-vs-1000n">
    <title>Comparing <m>n^{2}</m> and <m>1000n</m></title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell right="minor"><m>n</m></cell>
        <cell right="minor"><m>n^{2}</m></cell>
        <cell right="minor"><m>1000n</m></cell>
        <cell>Larger?</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell right="minor">10</cell>
        <cell right="minor">100</cell>
        <cell right="minor">10,000</cell>
        <cell><m>1000n</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell right="minor">100</cell>
        <cell right="minor">10,000</cell>
        <cell right="minor">100,000</cell>
        <cell><m>1000n</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell right="minor">1,000</cell>
        <cell right="minor">1,000,000</cell>
        <cell right="minor">1,000,000</cell>
        <cell>tie</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell right="minor">10,000</cell>
        <cell right="minor">100,000,000</cell>
        <cell right="minor">10,000,000</cell>
        <cell><m>n^{2}</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell right="minor">100,000</cell>
        <cell right="minor">10,000,000,000</cell>
        <cell right="minor">100,000,000</cell>
        <cell><m>n^{2}</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
  <p>For small <m>n</m> the coefficient 1000 makes the linear term look
            enormous. But once <m>n</m> passes 1,000, <m>n^{2}</m> overtakes
            <m>1000n</m> and never looks back&#x2014;by <m>n = 100{,}000</m>
            it is 100 times larger. This is exactly why computer scientists do
            not care about constant coefficients or lower-order terms: for the
            large inputs that matter in practice, the <em>shape</em> of the
            growth curve (quadratic vs.&#xA0;linear vs.&#xA0;constant) determines
            performance, not the exact multiplier in front.</p>
  <p>This insight leads us to <term>Big O notation</term>. The
            <term>order of magnitude</term> function describes the part of
            <m>T(n)</m> that increases the fastest as <m>n</m> increases.
            Order of magnitude is often called <term>Big O</term> (for
            <em>order</em>) and written as <m>O(f(n))</m>. It provides a useful
            approximation&#x2014;an <em>upper bound</em>&#x2014;on the actual
            number of steps in the computation. The function <m>f(n)</m>
            provides a simple representation of the dominant part of the
            original <m>T(n)</m>.</p>
  <p>More precisely, we say that <m>T(n)</m> is <m>O(f(n))</m> if there
            exist positive constants <m>c</m> and <m>n_0</m> such that
            <m>T(n) \leq c \cdot f(n)</m> for all <m>n \geq n_0</m>. In
            plain language, Big O gives an <em>upper bound</em> on growth: beyond
            some starting point, <m>T(n)</m> will never grow faster than a
            constant multiple of <m>f(n)</m>. We do not care about the exact
            multiplier or what happens for small inputs&#x2014;only the long-run
            growth trend matters.</p>
  <p>To go from <m>T(n)</m> to Big O, apply three rules:</p>
  <p><ol>
    <li><p><em>Keep only the dominant term.</em> The dominant term is the
        one that grows fastest as <m>n</m> increases. In
        <m>3n^{2} + 2n + 4</m>, the <m>n^{2}</m> term eventually
        dwarfs the others&#x2014;just as we saw with <m>n^{2}</m> vs.
        <m>1000n</m> in <xref ref="tbl-n2-vs-1000n"/>.</p></li>
    <li><p><em>Drop constant coefficients.</em> Since Big O describes
        the shape of the growth curve rather than an exact count,
        <m>3n^{2}</m> and <m>n^{2}</m> have the same Big O.</p></li>
    <li><p><em>Drop additive constants.</em> A flat amount of
        work, like <m>+4</m>, becomes irrelevant once <m>n</m>
        is large enough.</p></li>
  </ol></p>
  <p>Let us walk through two examples. First, <m>T(n) = 1 + n</m>
            (from <c>sum_of_n</c>). As <em>n</em> gets larger, the constant 1
            becomes less and less significant. We drop it and say the running
            time is <m>O(n)</m>. The 1 is still part of the exact count
            <m>T(n)</m>, but for large <em>n</em> our approximation is just as
            accurate without it.</p>
  <p>Second, suppose an algorithm has
            <m>T(n) = 5n^{2} + 27n + 1005</m>. When <em>n</em> is small, say 1
            or 2, the constant 1005 seems to dominate. However, as <em>n</em>
            gets larger, the <m>n^{2}</m> term becomes the most important. We
            drop the lower-order terms <m>27n</m> and <m>1005</m>, and then
            drop the coefficient <m>5</m>. The result:
            <m>O(n^{2})</m>.</p>
  <p>Returning to our worked example from
            <xref ref="algorithm-analysis_lst-dummycode"/>: we derived
            <m>T(n) = 3n^{2} + 2n + 4</m>. By looking at the exponents, we can
            easily see that the <m>n^{2}</m> term will be dominant and therefore
            this fragment of code is <m>O(n^{2})</m>. All of the other terms as
            well as the coefficient on the dominant term can be ignored as
            <em>n</em> grows larger.</p>
  <exercises>
    <title>Self Check</title>
    <exercise label="bigo_sc1">
      <statement>
        <p>An algorithm has a step count of
                    <m>T(n) = 3n + 100</m>. What is its Big-O classification?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>O(100)</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Although 100 is larger than 3n when n is small, as n grows
                        the 3n term will dominate. Constants are never the dominant
                        term when a variable term is present.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><m>O(n)</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct! The dominant term is <m>3n</m>. We drop the
                        coefficient 3 and the constant 100, leaving <m>O(n)</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>O(3n)</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>You correctly identified <m>3n</m> as the dominant term, but
                        in Big-O notation we also drop the constant coefficient.
                        <m>O(3n)</m> simplifies to <m>O(n)</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>O(3n + 100)</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Big-O captures only the dominant term, not the entire
                        expression. As <m>n</m> grows, which part of
                        <m>3n + 100</m> grows the fastest?</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
  <p>Although we do not see this in the summation example, sometimes the
            performance of an algorithm depends on the exact values of the data
            rather than simply the size of the problem. When this happens, a single
            <m>T(n)</m> function is not enough to tell the whole story. We need to
            characterize performance in terms of three scenarios:
            <term>best-case</term>, <term>worst-case</term>, and
            <term>average-case</term> performance.</p>
  <p>The <em>worst case</em> is the scenario in which the algorithm does the
            most work for a given input size. The <em>best case</em> is the
            scenario in which it does the least work. The <em>average case</em>
            represents the expected performance over all possible inputs of
            size <m>n</m>. Understanding these distinctions is important so that
            a single lucky (or unlucky) test does not mislead you about an
            algorithm's true behavior.</p>
  <p>For example, consider searching for a specific item in a list of
            <m>n</m> items by checking each one from the beginning. In the
            <em>best case</em>, the item is the very first one we check&#x2014;just
            1 operation. In the <em>worst case</em>, the item is last (or not in
            the list at all), requiring all <m>n</m> checks. On
            <em>average</em>, we would expect to check about <m>\frac{n}{2}</m>
            items. All three cases are <m>O(n)</m> in Big-O terms (since we drop
            the constant <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>), but the actual running time can
            vary significantly. When we say an algorithm is <m>O(n)</m>, we
            typically mean the <em>worst case</em> unless stated otherwise.</p>
  <p>A number of very common order of magnitude functions will come up over
            and over as you study algorithms. These are shown in <xref ref="algorithm-analysis_tbl-fntable"/>. In
            order to decide which of these functions is the dominant part of any
            <m>T(n)</m> function, we must see how they compare with one another
            as <em>n</em> gets large.</p>
  <table xml:id="algorithm-analysis_tbl-fntable">
    <tabular>
      <title>
        <term>Table 1: Common Functions for Big O</term>
      </title>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell>
          <term>f(n)</term>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <term>Name</term>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>1</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <term>Constant</term>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>\log n</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <term>Logarithmic</term>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>n</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <term>Linear</term>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>n\log n</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <term>Log linear</term>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>n^{2}</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <term>Quadratic</term>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>n^{3}</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <term>Cubic</term>
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>
          <m>2^{n}</m>
        </cell>
        <cell>
          <term>Exponential</term>
        </cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
  <p>To build intuition for these growth rates, here is a brief
            description of each:</p>
  <p><ul>
    <li><p><m>O(1)</m> &#x2013; <em>Constant:</em> The running time does not
        depend on the input size at all. Accessing an element in a list
        by its index is a constant-time operation.</p></li>
    <li><p><m>O(\log n)</m> &#x2013; <em>Logarithmic:</em> The running time
        grows very slowly because the algorithm repeatedly divides the
        problem in half. Binary search is the classic example: each
        comparison eliminates half of the remaining items, so doubling
        <m>n</m> adds only one extra step. If you are unfamiliar with
        logarithms, think of <m>\log_2 n</m> as <q>how many times can
        I divide <m>n</m> by 2 before reaching 1?</q> For
        <m>n = 1{,}000{,}000</m>, that is only about 20.</p></li>
    <li><p><m>O(n)</m> &#x2013; <em>Linear:</em> The running time grows in
        direct proportion to the input size. Scanning every element in
        a list once is linear.</p></li>
    <li><p><m>O(n \log n)</m> &#x2013; <em>Log-linear:</em> Slightly worse
        than linear. Many efficient sorting algorithms, such as merge
        sort, fall into this category.</p></li>
    <li><p><m>O(n^{2})</m> &#x2013; <em>Quadratic:</em> Common when an
        algorithm contains a pair of nested loops that each run
        <m>n</m> times. Simple sorting algorithms like bubble sort
        are quadratic.</p></li>
    <li><p><m>O(n^{3})</m> &#x2013; <em>Cubic:</em> Three levels of nested
        loops. Certain matrix multiplication algorithms are cubic.</p></li>
    <li><p><m>O(2^{n})</m> &#x2013; <em>Exponential:</em> The running time
        doubles with each additional input element. Algorithms that
        enumerate all subsets of a set are exponential and quickly
        become impractical.</p></li>
  </ul></p>
  <p>To see these growth rates in action, run the code in
            <xref ref="lst-growth-rates"/>. It computes the value of each
            common Big-O function for several input sizes and prints them
            side by side. Watch how the exponential column explodes while
            the logarithmic column barely moves.</p>
  <listing xml:id="lst-growth-rates"> <program label="lst-growth-rates" interactive="activecode" language="python">
    <input>
import math

print("%-8s %-10s %-10s %-10s %-12s %-14s %-14s %-14s" %
      ("n", "O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(n)", "O(n log n)",
       "O(n^2)", "O(n^3)", "O(2^n)"))
print("-" * 100)

for n in [1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100]:
    constant  = 1
    logarithm = round(math.log2(n), 2) if n > 0 else 0
    linear    = n
    nlogn     = round(n * math.log2(n), 2) if n > 0 else 0
    quadratic = n ** 2
    cubic     = n ** 3
    if n &lt;= 25:
        exponential = 2 ** n
    else:
        exponential = "too large!"

    print("%-8d %-10s %-10s %-10s %-12s %-14s %-14s %-14s" %
          (n, constant, logarithm, linear, nlogn,
           quadratic, cubic, exponential))
    </input>
  </program>
  </listing>
  <p>Notice that for <m>n = 100</m>, the quadratic function produces
            10,000 and the cubic produces 1,000,000, while <m>2^{n}</m> is so
            astronomically large it cannot even be displayed. Meanwhile,
            <m>\log n</m> is only about 6.6. This is why choosing the right
            algorithm matters&#x2014;the difference between <m>O(n)</m> and
            <m>O(n^{2})</m> can mean the difference between a program that
            finishes in seconds and one that takes hours.</p>
  <p><xref ref="i11_id2fig-graphfigure"/> shows graphs of the common
            functions from <xref ref="algorithm-analysis_tbl-fntable"/>. Notice that when <em>n</em> is small, the
            functions are not very well defined with respect to one another. It is
            hard to tell which is dominant. However, as <em>n</em> grows, there is a
            definite relationship and it is easy to see how they compare with one
            another.</p>
  <figure align="" xml:id="i11_id2fig-graphfigure">
    <caption xmlns:c="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:changeset="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:citation="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:cpp="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:index="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:js="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:math="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:py="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:rst="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:std="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/">Figure 1: Plot of Common Big O Functions</caption>
    <image source="AlgorithmAnalysis/Figures/newplot.png" width="50%"/>
  </figure>
  <exercises>
    <title>Self Check</title>
    <exercise label="bigo_sc2">
      <statement>
        <p>An algorithm has a step count of
                    <m>T(n) = 2n^{3} + n^{2} + 5n + 10</m>. What is its Big-O?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>O(n^{2})</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Look at the exponents. The <m>n^{2}</m> term is not the
                        highest power of <m>n</m> in this expression.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><m>O(n^{3})</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct! The term <m>2n^{3}</m> has the highest exponent, so
                        it dominates as <m>n</m> grows. We drop the coefficient 2
                        and all lower-order terms, giving <m>O(n^{3})</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>O(2n^{3})</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>You found the right dominant term, but remember to drop the
                        constant coefficient. <m>O(2n^{3})</m> simplifies to
                        <m>O(n^{3})</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>O(n)</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The linear term <m>5n</m> is present, but it is not the
                        dominant term. Look for the term with the highest power
                        of <m>n</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
  <figure align="" xml:id="fig-graphfigure2">
    <caption xmlns:c="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:changeset="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:citation="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:cpp="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:index="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:js="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:math="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:py="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:rst="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/" xmlns:std="https://www.sphinx-doc.org/">Figure 2: Comparing <m>T(n)</m> with Common Big O Functions</caption>
    <image source="AlgorithmAnalysis/Figures/newplot2.png" width="50%"/>
  </figure>
  <p><xref ref="fig-graphfigure2"/> shows a few of the common Big O functions as they
            compare with the <m>T(n)</m> function from
            <xref ref="algorithm-analysis_lst-dummycode"/>. Note that
            <m>T(n)</m> is initially larger than the cubic function. However, as
            n grows, the cubic function quickly overtakes <m>T(n)</m>. It is easy
            to see that <m>T(n)</m> then follows the quadratic function as
            <m>n</m> continues to grow.</p>
  <exercises>
    <title>Self Check</title>
    <exercise label="bigo_sc3">
      <statement>
        <p>Consider the following code fragment. What is its Big-O?</p>
        <program language="python">
          <input>
for i in range(n):
    for j in range(n):
        k = 2 + 2
print(k)
          </input>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>O(n)</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>There are two nested loops, each running <m>n</m> times.
                        That gives <m>n \times n</m> iterations of the inner
                        statement.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><m>O(n^{2})</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct! The nested loops produce <m>n^{2}</m> iterations.
                        The <c>print</c> statement runs once (<m>O(1)</m>), which is
                        insignificant compared to <m>n^{2}</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>O(n^{3})</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>There are only two nested loops, not three. Count the
                        levels of nesting carefully.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>O(1)</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The loops depend on <m>n</m>, so the running time cannot be
                        constant. As <m>n</m> grows, the number of iterations
                        grows too.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="bigo_sc4">
      <statement>
        <p>Write two Python functions to find the minimum number in a list.
                    The first function should compare each number to every other number
                    on the list <m>O(n^{2})</m>. The second function should be linear
                    <m>O(n)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <program label="bigo_sc4_editor" interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <input>
# O(n^2) solution: compare every pair
def find_min_quadratic(lst):
    # TODO: For each element, compare it against every other element.
    # If no other element is smaller, it is the minimum.
    pass


# O(n) solution: single pass through the list
def find_min_linear(lst):
    # TODO: Start by assuming the first element is the minimum.
    # Walk through the rest of the list, updating whenever you
    # find something smaller.
    pass


test_list = [5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 2, 7]
print("Quadratic:", find_min_quadratic(test_list))
print("Linear:", find_min_linear(test_list))
        </input>
        <tests>
import unittest

class TestFindMin(unittest.TestCase):

    def test_1_quadratic_basic(self):
        """Test 1 - Quadratic: does it find the minimum in a typical list?"""
        result = find_min_quadratic([5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 2, 7])
        self.assertEqual(result, 1,
            "\n  find_min_quadratic([5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 2, 7]) returned %s instead of 1."
            "\n  Hint: For each element, check if any other element is smaller."
            " If none is smaller, that element is the minimum." % result)

    def test_2_linear_basic(self):
        """Test 2 - Linear: does it find the minimum in a typical list?"""
        result = find_min_linear([5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 2, 7])
        self.assertEqual(result, 1,
            "\n  find_min_linear([5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 2, 7]) returned %s instead of 1."
            "\n  Hint: Start with the first element as your current minimum."
            " Loop through the rest and update whenever you find a smaller value." % result)

    def test_3_single_element(self):
        """Test 3 - Both functions: single-element list"""
        q = find_min_quadratic([42])
        l = find_min_linear([42])
        self.assertEqual(q, 42,
            "\n  find_min_quadratic([42]) returned %s instead of 42." % q)
        self.assertEqual(l, 42,
            "\n  find_min_linear([42]) returned %s instead of 42." % l)

    def test_4_negative_numbers(self):
        """Test 4 - Both functions: list with negative numbers"""
        q = find_min_quadratic([10, -5, 3, -1])
        l = find_min_linear([10, -5, 3, -1])
        self.assertEqual(q, -5,
            "\n  find_min_quadratic([10, -5, 3, -1]) returned %s instead of -5." % q)
        self.assertEqual(l, -5,
            "\n  find_min_linear([10, -5, 3, -1]) returned %s instead of -5." % l)

    def test_5_min_at_boundaries(self):
        """Test 5 - Both functions: minimum at start and end of list"""
        q_first = find_min_quadratic([1, 5, 9])
        l_last  = find_min_linear([9, 5, 1])
        self.assertEqual(q_first, 1,
            "\n  find_min_quadratic([1, 5, 9]) returned %s instead of 1."
            "\n  Hint: Make sure you check the first element too." % q_first)
        self.assertEqual(l_last, 1,
            "\n  find_min_linear([9, 5, 1]) returned %s instead of 1."
            "\n  Hint: Make sure you check all elements including the last." % l_last)

unittest.main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
  <p>Now that we have a formal notation for describing algorithmic
            efficiency, we can apply it to more interesting problems. In the
            next section, we will examine several solutions to the classic
            <em>anagram detection</em> problem. Each solution uses a different
            strategy&#x2014;and as we will see, those strategies lead to very
            different Big-O classifications, from quadratic all the way down
            to linear.</p>
  <video xml:id="findMinVid" youtube="p0COF_m6H1c" width="auto"/>
  <subsection xml:id="algorithm-analysis_practice-big-o">
    <title>Practice: Comparing Quadratic vs.&#xA0;Linear Solutions</title>
    <p>In this exercise you will implement two different algorithms that
              solve the same problem&#x2014;checking whether a list contains any
              duplicate values&#x2014;and then measure the performance difference
              between them.</p>
    <p>The <em>brute-force</em> approach uses two nested loops to compare
              every element against every other element. Because there are
              <m>n</m> elements and each is compared to every other, this
              strategy performs roughly <m>n^{2}</m> comparisons, making it
              <m>O(n^{2})</m>.</p>
    <p>A smarter approach takes advantage of a Python <c>set</c>. A set
              is a collection that does not allow duplicate values, and
              checking whether an item is already in a set is an <m>O(1)</m>
              operation on average. By walking through the list once and
              checking each element against a set of previously seen values,
              we can detect duplicates in <m>O(n)</m> time.</p>
    <p>Your tasks are:</p>
    <p><ol>
      <li><p>Complete <c>has_duplicates_quadratic</c> using nested loops.
              Return <c>True</c> if any two different positions contain the
              same value, <c>False</c> otherwise.</p></li>
      <li><p>Complete <c>has_duplicates_linear</c> using a set. Walk through
              the list, and for each element check if it is already in the
              set. If it is, return <c>True</c>. Otherwise, add it to the
              set. If you finish the loop without finding a duplicate,
              return <c>False</c>.</p></li>
      <li><p>In <c>main</c>, use <c>time.time()</c> to measure and print
              the elapsed time for each approach.</p></li>
    </ol></p>
    <p>Hidden test cases will check both functions for correctness when
              you run your code.</p>
    <listing xml:id="lst-practice-big-o"> <program label="lst-practice-big-o" interactive="activecode" language="python">
      <input>
import time
import random


def populate_collection(size):
    collection = []
    for i in range(size):
        random_value = random.randint(1, size * 10)
        collection.append(random_value)
    return collection


def has_duplicates_quadratic(lst):
    # TODO: Use two nested loops to compare every pair of elements.
    # If lst[i] == lst[j] and i != j, return True.
    # If no duplicates are found, return False.
    pass


def has_duplicates_linear(lst):
    # TODO: Create an empty set. Loop through the list.
    # If the current element is already in the set, return True.
    # Otherwise, add it to the set.
    # If you finish the loop, return False.
    pass


def main():
    collection_size = 5000
    collection = populate_collection(collection_size)

    # TODO: Record start time, call has_duplicates_quadratic,
    #       record end time, and print the elapsed time.

    # TODO: Record start time, call has_duplicates_linear,
    #       record end time, and print the elapsed time.

    pass


main()
      </input>
      <tests>
import unittest

class TestHasDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):

    def test_1_quadratic_with_duplicates(self):
        """Test 1 - Quadratic: detects duplicates when present"""
        result = has_duplicates_quadratic([1, 2, 3, 2, 5])
        self.assertTrue(result,
            "\n  has_duplicates_quadratic([1, 2, 3, 2, 5]) returned %s instead of True."
            "\n  Hint: Compare each element at index i with every element at index j"
            " where j > i. Return True when you find a match." % result)

    def test_2_quadratic_no_duplicates(self):
        """Test 2 - Quadratic: returns False when no duplicates exist"""
        result = has_duplicates_quadratic([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])
        self.assertFalse(result,
            "\n  has_duplicates_quadratic([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]) returned %s instead of False."
            "\n  Hint: Make sure you only return True when two DIFFERENT positions"
            " hold the same value. Return False after checking all pairs." % result)

    def test_3_linear_with_duplicates(self):
        """Test 3 - Linear: detects duplicates when present"""
        result = has_duplicates_linear([10, 20, 30, 20, 50])
        self.assertTrue(result,
            "\n  has_duplicates_linear([10, 20, 30, 20, 50]) returned %s instead of True."
            "\n  Hint: As you loop, check if each element is already in your set."
            " If it is, return True immediately." % result)

    def test_4_linear_no_duplicates(self):
        """Test 4 - Linear: returns False when no duplicates exist"""
        result = has_duplicates_linear([10, 20, 30, 40, 50])
        self.assertFalse(result,
            "\n  has_duplicates_linear([10, 20, 30, 40, 50]) returned %s instead of False."
            "\n  Hint: If you finish the loop without finding any element already"
            " in the set, return False." % result)

    def test_5_edge_cases(self):
        """Test 5 - Edge cases: empty list and single element"""
        q_empty  = has_duplicates_quadratic([])
        l_empty  = has_duplicates_linear([])
        q_single = has_duplicates_quadratic([7])
        l_single = has_duplicates_linear([7])
        self.assertFalse(q_empty,
            "\n  has_duplicates_quadratic([]) should return False for an empty list.")
        self.assertFalse(l_empty,
            "\n  has_duplicates_linear([]) should return False for an empty list.")
        self.assertFalse(q_single,
            "\n  has_duplicates_quadratic([7]) should return False for a single element.")
        self.assertFalse(l_single,
            "\n  has_duplicates_linear([7]) should return False for a single element.")

unittest.main()
      </tests>
    </program>
    </listing>
    <p>After your implementation passes all tests, try increasing
              <c>collection_size</c> to 10000, 20000, and 40000. Each time
              you double the size, the quadratic version should take roughly
              four times as long, while the linear version should take
              roughly twice as long. This is the power of Big-O analysis
              in action&#x2014;choosing the right algorithm can make a dramatic
              difference.</p>
  </subsection>
